# The SHA-256 certificate fingerprint Google signs the app with, when Play App Signing re-signs your bundle.
# Play Console → Test and release → App integrity → App signing key certificate → "SHA-256 certificate fingerprint".
#
# Paste it alone on the line below — with or without colons, in either case; scripts/assetlinks.mjs normalises it.
# Nothing is published until a line here is a real fingerprint (32 bytes of hex), and a paste that is not one is
# ignored rather than published: a malformed entry leaves Android comparing against nothing, so the links keep
# opening a browser with nothing looking broken.
#
# Why this file exists: an app installed from the site is signed with android/'s key and matches the fingerprint the
# build publishes by itself. An app installed from Play carries Google's certificate instead, so it only opens
# Saligue links while Google's fingerprint is listed too. Both live in public/.well-known/assetlinks.json; a rebuild
# only ever adds to that list, never replaces it.
#
# A build can also be given one without touching this file: SALIGUE_PLAY_SHA256=AA:BB:… node scripts/build-app.mjs --aab
